Software the installer fetches, rather than ships
  The installer downloads these at install time, checks them against a pinned checksum or a lockfile hash, and runs them under
  their own licenses; our downloads do not contain them:
    - Node.js (pinned version, from nodejs.org): MIT, with its bundled third-party components under their own licenses.
    - The engine's and the sandbox provider's npm dependencies, from their lockfiles (see THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md), including:
        . the embedded PostgreSQL binaries (PostgreSQL License);
        . the sharp image library's libvips builds (LGPL-3.0-or-later): used as a separate, replaceable library;
        . @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk (with its per-platform package that contains a native "claude" program of about
          215 MB), @cursor/sdk (and its platform package) and @github/copilot-<platform>, which declare "SEE LICENSE IN ..."
          and are under their owners' own terms (Anthropic's legal agreements, Cursor's terms of service, GitHub's), not an
          open-source license. The engine's agent adapters depend on them, we do not. Our downloads contain none of them:
          the release holds only engine/package.json and engine/package-lock.json, and the installer fetches these from
          registry.npmjs.org onto the host, where each runs under its own terms. Anyone who redistributes an image that
          bundles them must read and follow those terms first.
        . the opencode-<platform> packages (prebuilt "opencode" programs of about 180 MB each): the packages declare no license
          and carry no license file, so THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md lists them as UNKNOWN. The upstream project is published
          under the MIT License; the packages themselves do not say so. Treat them like the proprietary ones above before
          redistributing.
        . busboy, ssh2, cpu-features and buildcheck also appear as UNKNOWN: their package.json uses the old "licenses" array
          (MIT, with a LICENSE file in each package), which the generator does not read.

If you build an image that bundles the engine
  Redistributing the engine's installed packages (an installer image, a container image) makes you the distributor of every
  package above. Check each license in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md, keep every notice, offer the source of copyleft components, and
  read the terms of the packages that say "SEE LICENSE IN ...".
